item.content:encoded:
&lt;h1&gt;


Adding a Mobile‑First TabBar to the Albaida‑Tickets Dashboard (React + Next.js)
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; I added a client‑side &lt;code&gt;TabBar.tsx&lt;/code&gt; component to make the Albaida‑Tickets admin UI usable on phones, wiring it to Next.js routing and styling it with Tailwind. The change lives in &lt;code&gt;components/TabBar.tsx&lt;/code&gt; and is referenced from &lt;code&gt;pages/dashboard.tsx&lt;/code&gt;, fixing the “content overflows on mobile” symptom.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Albaida‑Tickets dashboard was built as a desktop‑only grid of cards. When I opened the page on an iPhone X, the layout overflowed horizontally, the filter sidebar never collapsed, and the bottom navigation was missing. The console showed no errors, but the UI was unusable:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Viewport width: 375px
Dashboard container width: 1024px → horizontal scroll
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Because the app is a Next.js project with server‑rendered pages, I couldn’t just drop a CSS media query on the existing layout; the navigation needed a mobile‑specific component that works on the client (uses &lt;code&gt;next/link&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;useRouter&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first attempt was to hide the existing sidebar with a simple Tailwind utility (&lt;code&gt;hidden md:block&lt;/code&gt;) and rely on the top navigation for navigation. That approach failed for two reasons:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No persistent navigation:&lt;/strong&gt; Users lost quick access to the main sections (&lt;code&gt;Tickets&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Analytics&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Settings&lt;/code&gt;).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Route detection broke:&lt;/strong&gt; The active state was tied to the server‑side layout, so the highlighted link never updated after a client‑side navigation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also tried injecting a pure CSS “bottom bar” via a global stylesheet, but because the component needed to read the current route to apply the active style, the static CSS couldn’t react to client navigation.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Create the TabBar component
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;File: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;components/TabBar.tsx&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

