&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;nav&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Key points&lt;/em&gt;  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Client‑side routing&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;useRouter&lt;/code&gt; gives us the current path, enabling the active style without a full page reload.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tailwind utilities&lt;/strong&gt;: The component is hidden on &lt;code&gt;md&lt;/code&gt; and larger (&lt;code&gt;md:hidden&lt;/code&gt;) so it only appears on phones.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;clsx&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: Keeps conditional class logic readable.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Wire the component into the dashboard layout
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;File: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;pages/dashboard.tsx&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; (excerpt)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DashboardGrid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@/components/DashboardGrid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;TabBar&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@/components/TabBar&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;DashboardPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"pt-4 pb-20 md:pb-4"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DashboardGrid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* Mobile navigation */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;TabBar&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I placed &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;TabBar /&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; after the main content so it stays fixed at the bottom. The &lt;code&gt;pb-20&lt;/code&gt; on the &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;main&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; element reserves space for the bar on mobile.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Update the changelog and metadata
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The weekly automation script adds a new entry to &lt;code&gt;content/2026/10/24/albaida-tickets/changelog.md&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight markdown"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gu"&gt;## [2026-10-24] albaida-tickets&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="gu"&gt;### Added&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`components/TabBar.tsx`&lt;/span&gt; – new bottom navigation for mobile devices (client component with Link, route detection and Tailwind styling)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And flips the generation flags in &lt;code&gt;content/2026/10/24/albaida-tickets/metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gd"&gt;-  "medium_generated": false,
-  "substack_generated": false,
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "medium_generated": true,
+  "substack_generated": true,
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;These changes are consumed by the content‑automation pipeline that builds the weekly newsletter and the Dev.to post you’re reading now.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. Verify the fix
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Running the dev server (&lt;code&gt;npm run dev&lt;/code&gt;) and opening the page on a Chrome device emulator showed:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No horizontal scroll.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The TabBar appears at the bottom, icons highlight correctly when navigating.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The desktop layout remains untouched (&lt;code&gt;md&lt;/code&gt; breakpoint).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also added a quick Cypress test (`c&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Part of my &lt;a href="https://dev.to/zaerohell"&gt;Build in Public&lt;/a&gt; series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Repo: &lt;code&gt;zaerohell/content-automation&lt;/code&gt; · 2026-10-25&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;#playadev #buildinpublic&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My Surface Laptop Ultra decodes short code replies from qwen3-coder:30b at &lt;strong&gt;90.9 tokens a second&lt;/strong&gt;, twice the 44.7 that &lt;a href="https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/AppsonAzureBlog/qwen3-8-27b-on-a-lenovo-laptop-run-it-on-rtx-spark-watch-it-in-azure-monitor/4567828" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Microsoft's Lenovo Yoga 9n review&lt;/a&gt; measured for Qwen3.8-27B on the same chip. Both laptops use NVIDIA's RTX Spark N1X with 64 GB of LPDDR5X-9400. I repeated that post's two benchmarks with Ollama on Windows: no Docker, no vLLM, no speculative decoding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The long prompt tells a different story. With a 100,589-token prompt, qwen3-coder took 146 seconds to its first token and then decoded at 19.7 tokens a second, where the Lenovo setup held 44.3. Over the whole run the GPU averaged 33 W and peaked at 75 °C.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


At a glance
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Verdict:&lt;/strong&gt; with Ollama alone, the Surface Laptop Ultra is a fast machine for short, interactive coding requests with a local model. For a coding agent that resends a 100K-token conversation on every turn, the Lenovo post's vLLM setup is the better pattern.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I liked:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Setup is one signed installer and one &lt;code&gt;ollama pull&lt;/code&gt;. Nothing runs in WSL or Docker.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;qwen3-coder:30b (30.5B parameters, 8 of 128 experts active per token) and a 102,400-token context fit entirely on the GPU: 26.7 GiB.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;90.9 tokens a second on short code prompts, steady across three runs (88–92).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;qwen3:8b, the smaller model, decodes at 44–50 tokens a second in every run I made.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Good to know:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Decode speed falls about 4.6 times between a 20-token and a 100K-token prompt, because the attention cache grows with every token.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Prompt processing ran at 687 tokens a second at 100K tokens, two-thirds of the Lenovo setup's 1,033.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ollama silently cuts a prompt longer than its context window to about half. Only its server log says so.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who it's for:&lt;/strong&gt; developers who want a local coding model for chat, edits and short agent turns, with no per-token bill and no container stack.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The machine
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Surface Laptop Ultra has the same chip and memory as the Lenovo in Microsoft's post, so the hardware comparison is like for like.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Microsoft Surface Laptop Ultra, as tested&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Chip&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;NVIDIA RTX Spark N1X: 20-core Arm CPU, 6,144-core Blackwell GPU, plus an NVIDIA NPU&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Memory&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;64 GB of Samsung LPDDR5X-9400, shared by the CPU and the GPU&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;GPU's share&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;31,232 MiB dedicated to the GPU; Windows sees the other 32.2 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Software&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Windows 11 Pro on Arm (build 28000), NVIDIA driver 617.42, Ollama 0.40.11 (native Windows ARM64, per-user install)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Power&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;On AC power, Windows Balanced power plan&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Models&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;qwen3-coder:30b and qwen3:8b, both Q4_K_M, stored on the internal SSD&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The GPU's share is the same 32 GB split the Lenovo post used. With qwen3-coder and its 102,400-token cache loaded, GPU memory peaked at 29,952 of 31,232 MiB.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How I measured
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I copied the Lenovo post's two single-request benchmarks as closely as Ollama allows; the agent session and Azure Monitor parts I left out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Code benchmark&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Long-prompt benchmark&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Task&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A thread-safe LRU cache in Python with tests, a token-bucket rate limiter in TypeScript, Dijkstra's algorithm in Go&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One question after a long prompt cut from Windows PowerShell's own format and module files&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Prompt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;18–21 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;100,589 tokens (qwen3-coder) or 37,151 (qwen3:8b, whose limit is 40,960), with a random first line so nothing came from the prompt cache&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reply&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;512 tokens each, temperature 0, seed 42, thinking off&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;384 tokens, temperature 0, thinking off&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Context&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;102,400 tokens (qwen3-coder), 40,960 (qwen3:8b)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Same&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;How it was sent&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One request at a time to Ollama's &lt;code&gt;/api/generate&lt;/code&gt;, after a warm-up load&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One request, after a calibration prompt that measured characters per token&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The script, &lt;code&gt;speed-bench.ps1&lt;/code&gt;, unloads every model first, records Ollama's own token timings and samples GPU power, temperature, clock and memory with &lt;code&gt;nvidia-smi&lt;/code&gt; once a second. Decode speed is Ollama's &lt;code&gt;eval_count / eval_duration&lt;/code&gt;; prompt speed is &lt;code&gt;prompt_eval_count / prompt_eval_duration&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One lesson from the first attempt: my first long prompt for qwen3-coder came out at 105,530 tokens, over its 102,400-token window, and Ollama kept only the last 51,202 without returning an error. The script now calibrates on a large slice of the text and flags any prompt that comes back short.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Results
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On short prompts, qwen3-coder:30b is the fastest setup on either laptop; at 100K tokens it is the slowest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fcbzbccxrz1xqdyk5xeon.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fcbzbccxrz1xqdyk5xeon.png" alt="Decode speed: qwen3-coder 90.9 tok/s short, 19.7 at 100,589 tokens; qwen3:8b 45.1 short, 22.3 at 37,151 tokens; Qwen3.8-27B on vLLM 44.7 short, 44.3 at 97,880 tokens" width="800" height="412"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The three code prompts landed close together: 92.1, 90.2 and 90.5 tokens a second for qwen3-coder, and 46.5, 45.1 and 43.8 for qwen3:8b. The first token of a short reply arrived in 0.08–0.19 seconds once the model was loaded.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reading the long prompt is where the wait comes from:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Setup&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Prompt&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Prompt processing&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;First token&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;qwen3-coder:30b, Ollama&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;100,589 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;687 tok/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;146 s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;qwen3:8b, Ollama&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;37,151 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,492 tok/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;25 s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Qwen3.8-27B, vLLM on the Lenovo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;97,880 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,033 tok/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;about 95 s, worked out from its prompt speed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a chat that grows turn by turn, Ollama reuses the cached start of the conversation, so only the new part is read. The 146 seconds is the cost of a brand-new 100K-token prompt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Power, heat and clocks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The GPU slows its clock while it reads a long prompt and speeds back up to reply, so the lower clock tracks the work, not heat building up.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fqvu1abveup331v88ivec.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fqvu1abveup331v88ivec.png" alt="GPU power, temperature and graphics clock over the qwen3-coder run: about 34 W and 1,656 MHz while reading the 100K prompt, 2,260 MHz while replying, 60 W and 75 °C peaks" width="799" height="521"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reading a prompt is compute-bound: after a short burst to 60 W, the GPU settled near 34 W and dropped its clock to about 1.65 GHz for the whole 146 seconds. Replying is memory-bound: the clock went back up to about 2.25 GHz at 30 W, and the temperature fell from 71 to 64 °C. That pattern looks like a power limit, not thermal throttling.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 75 °C peak is 8 °C above the Lenovo's three-hour peak, reached in a run of about four minutes. I haven't tested sustained load yet, so treat this as a first look at the Surface's cooling, not a verdict.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why long context costs so much here
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Decode on this chip is a question of bytes, as the Lenovo post showed: every token reads the active weights plus the whole attention cache, at roughly 235 GB/s.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;tokens per second ≈ memory bandwidth ÷ bytes read per token
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;qwen3-coder's mixture of experts makes the weights cheap: only about 3.3B of its 30.5B parameters run per token, about 2 GB at Q4_K_M. Its cache is the expensive part. Each token keeps keys and values for 48 layers × 4 heads × 128 dimensions in 16-bit floats, 96 KiB a token, so 100,000 tokens of cache is about 9.9 GB.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Run&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Weights read per token&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Cache read per token&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Measured decode&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Implied bandwidth&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;qwen3-coder:30b, short prompt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~2.0 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~0.05 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;90.9 tok/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~185 GB/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;qwen3-coder:30b, 100,589-token prompt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~2.0 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~9.9 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;19.7 tok/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~235 GB/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;qwen3:8b, short prompt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~5.1 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~0.08 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;45.1 tok/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~235 GB/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;qwen3:8b, 37,151-token prompt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~5.1 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~5.5 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;22.3 tok/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~235 GB/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three of the four runs imply the same 235 GB/s, close to the 229 GB/s the Lenovo post measured with a read test. The short qwen3-coder run falls short of it because routing tokens between experts adds work that isn't memory reads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The weights and cache sizes are my estimates from the model files and their architecture, not measurements. They explain the gap with the Lenovo setup: Qwen3.8-27B keeps a fixed-size state in 48 of its 64 layers, so its cache barely grows, and speculative decoding produces about five tokens per read of the weights.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Laptop vs laptop
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hardware matches, so the differences come from the software stack and the model. Read this as two working setups, not a controlled benchmark.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Surface Laptop Ultra (this post)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Lenovo Yoga 9n 2-in-1 (&lt;a href="https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/AppsonAzureBlog/qwen3-8-27b-on-a-lenovo-laptop-run-it-on-rtx-spark-watch-it-in-azure-monitor/4567828" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Microsoft's post&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Chip and memory&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;RTX Spark N1X, 64 GB LPDDR5X-9400, 32 GB to the GPU&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Same&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Model&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;qwen3-coder:30b, mixture of experts, ~3.3B active&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Qwen3.8-27B, dense, hybrid attention&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Weights&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Q4_K_M, 17.3 GiB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;NVFP4, 19.1 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Server&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Ollama 0.40.11, native on Windows&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;vLLM 0.31 in Docker Desktop on WSL 2&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Speculative decoding&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;None&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;DFlash 2 drafter, 7 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Context used&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;102,400 tokens (the model supports 262,144)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;262,144 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Setup effort&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One installer, one model pull&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Docker, a custom image, WSL memory caps, LiteLLM&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Monitoring&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;nvidia-smi samples to CSV&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Azure Monitor, Managed Grafana, alert rules&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Best for&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Chat, edits and short agent turns&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Long agent sessions with 100K+ token conversations&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Lenovo post also ran three hours of OpenCode traffic at about 27 tokens a second per request. I haven't run an agent session on the Surface yet, so there is no matching number here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What runs where, and how to set it up
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The whole stack is one Windows service in front of the GPU; there is no container, gateway, or collector to run.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fvh5mragn1ms2ul2jw3x4.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fvh5mragn1ms2ul2jw3x4.png" alt="The stack: VS Code, an OpenAI-style client and speed-bench.ps1 call Ollama on localhost:11434, which runs llama-server on the RTX Spark GPU; models load from the internal SSD; nvidia-smi samples feed the results" width="799" height="471"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ollama starts a llama.cpp server per loaded model and offloads every layer to the GPU. The GPU already had a 32 GB share of the unified memory; Microsoft's post says the NVIDIA app's Dedicated GPU Memory setting controls it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Check the GPU: &lt;code&gt;nvidia-smi&lt;/code&gt; should list the RTX Spark N1X and its memory.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Install Ollama with its signed script: &lt;code&gt;irm https://ollama.com/install.ps1 | iex&lt;/code&gt;. It installs per user, with no admin rights needed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pull the models: &lt;code&gt;ollama pull qwen3-coder:30b&lt;/code&gt; (17.3 GiB) and &lt;code&gt;ollama pull qwen3:8b&lt;/code&gt; (4.9 GiB). Keep them on the internal SSD.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Set a default context for editors: the user variable &lt;code&gt;OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH=65536&lt;/code&gt;, then restart Ollama. The benchmark sets its own context per request.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run the benchmark from the folder that holds it: &lt;code&gt;powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\speed-bench.ps1&lt;/code&gt; (see PowerShell at bottom of the post), or add &lt;code&gt;-Models qwen3-coder:30b&lt;/code&gt; for one model. Running both models takes about seven minutes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Point VS Code at it: Continue's &lt;code&gt;config.yaml&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;provider: ollama&lt;/code&gt;, or the Ollama provider in Copilot Chat's language models.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Rules of thumb
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Count bytes per token.&lt;/strong&gt; On RTX Spark, decode speed is about 235 GB/s divided by the weights and cache each token reads.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pick a mixture-of-experts model for short turns.&lt;/strong&gt; Reading 2 GB of weights per token instead of 5 GB is what doubles qwen3-coder's speed over qwen3:8b.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Budget for the cache at long context.&lt;/strong&gt; At 100K tokens, qwen3-coder's 16-bit cache is five times the size of its active weights.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Check the prompt count.&lt;/strong&gt; If &lt;code&gt;prompt_eval_count&lt;/code&gt; comes back far below what you sent, Ollama cut your prompt to fit the context window.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Watch the clock, not just the temperature.&lt;/strong&gt; During prompt processing, the GPU held about 1.65 GHz at 34 W; during replies, it ran at 2.25 GHz.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The bottom line
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Surface Laptop Ultra has the same chip and memory as the Lenovo in Microsoft's post, so the same models and limits apply. With nothing more than Ollama, qwen3-coder:30b answers short coding requests at 91 tokens a second, with the first token in under 0.2 seconds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For 100K-token agent sessions, Ollama on this machine is the weak spot: a fresh prompt waits more than two minutes and replies drop to 20 tokens a second. Next, I want to try an 8-bit KV cache (&lt;code&gt;OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE=q8_0&lt;/code&gt;), which should roughly halve the cache's bytes, and a three-hour agent session to compare with the Lenovo's 27 tokens a second.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Powershell&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;# Ollama version of the speed benchmark from Microsoft's "Qwen3.8-27B on a Lenovo laptop" post:
#  - code benchmark: three code-generation prompts, 512-token replies, temperature 0, thinking off, one at a time
#  - long-prompt benchmark: one question after a long prompt whose random first line defeats the prefix cache, 384-token reply
# GPU power, temperature, clock and memory are sampled every second with nvidia-smi during the whole run.
# Writes speed-bench-&amp;lt;COMPUTER&amp;gt;-&amp;lt;stamp&amp;gt;.json and .gpu.csv in the current directory.
param(
[string[]]$Models = @('qwen3:8b', 'qwen3-coder:30b'),
[string]$Api = 'http://localhost:11434'
)
$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'

# Context per model: big enough for the long prompt plus reply, within the model's maximum and the GPU's memory
$plan = @{
'qwen3:8b'        = @{ num_ctx = 40960;  long_tokens = 36000 }   # qwen3:8b's trained maximum is 40,960
'qwen3-coder:30b' = @{ num_ctx = 102400; long_tokens = 97880 }   # the article's long prompt was 97,880 tokens
}
$codePrompts = @(
'Write a thread-safe LRU cache in Python with unit tests.',
'Write a token-bucket rate limiter in TypeScript.',
"Implement Dijkstra's shortest-path algorithm in Go."
)

function Post-Ollama($path, $body) {
Invoke-RestMethod -Method Post -Uri ($Api + $path) -ContentType 'application/json' -Body ($body | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 8) -TimeoutSec 1800
}
function Unload-All {
foreach ($m in @((Invoke-RestMethod ($Api + '/api/ps')).models)) { Post-Ollama '/api/generate' @{ model = $m.name; keep_alive = 0 } | Out-Null }
Start-Sleep 3
}
function Measure-Reply($r, $wall) {
[ordered]@{
prompt_tokens       = $r.prompt_eval_count
prefill_tok_s       = if ($r.prompt_eval_duration) { [math]::Round($r.prompt_eval_count / ($r.prompt_eval_duration / 1e9), 1) } else { $null }
output_tokens       = $r.eval_count
decode_tok_s        = if ($r.eval_duration) { [math]::Round($r.eval_count / ($r.eval_duration / 1e9), 2) } else { $null }
first_token_s       = [math]::Round(($r.load_duration + $r.prompt_eval_duration) / 1e9, 2)
wall_s              = [math]::Round($wall, 2)
done_reason         = $r.done_reason
}
}
function Invoke-Timed($body) {
$sw = [Diagnostics.Stopwatch]::StartNew(); $r = Post-Ollama '/api/generate' $body; $sw.Stop()
return $r, $sw.Elapsed.TotalSeconds
}

# Text for the long prompt: PowerShell's own format/type files and module sources, present on every Windows machine
$corpus = (Get-ChildItem "$env:windir\System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0" -Recurse -File -Include *.ps1xml, *.psm1, *.psd1 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
Sort-Object FullName | ForEach-Object { "`n### FILE: $($_.Name)`n" + [IO.File]::ReadAllText($_.FullName) }) -join ''

$stamp = Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd-HHmmss'
$base = Join-Path (Get-Location) "speed-bench-$env:COMPUTERNAME-$stamp"
$gpuCsv = "$base.gpu.csv"
$gpuInfo = (&amp;amp; nvidia-smi -i 0 --query-gpu=name,driver_version,memory.total --format=csv,noheader).Trim()
$sampler = Start-Process nvidia-smi -ArgumentList '-i 0 --query-gpu=timestamp,power.draw,temperature.gpu,clocks.gr,memory.used --format=csv,noheader,nounits -l 1 -f', "`"$gpuCsv`"" -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru
$phases = @()
$results = @()

try {
foreach ($model in $Models) {
$p = $plan[$model]
if (-not $p) { $p = @{ num_ctx = 32768; long_tokens = 30000 } }
$caps = @((Post-Ollama '/api/show' @{ model = $model }).capabilities)
$opts = { param($n) @{ num_ctx = $p.num_ctx; num_predict = $n; temperature = 0; seed = 42 } }
function New-Body($prompt, $n) {
$b = @{ model = $model; prompt = $prompt; stream = $false; keep_alive = '15m'; options = (&amp;amp; $opts $n) }
if ($caps -contains 'thinking') { $b.think = $false }   # thinking off, as in the article; non-thinking models reject the flag
$b
}

Write-Host "=== $model (num_ctx $($p.num_ctx)) ==="
Unload-All
$t0 = Get-Date
$r, $w = Invoke-Timed (New-Body 'Reply with exactly: warm' 8)
$loaded = @((Invoke-RestMethod ($Api + '/api/ps')).models | Where-Object name -eq $model)[0]
$phases += [pscustomobject]@{ model = $model; phase = 'load'; start = $t0; end = Get-Date }
$modelInfo = [ordered]@{
model = $model; num_ctx = $p.num_ctx; loaded_context = $loaded.context_length
load_s = [math]::Round($r.load_duration / 1e9, 1)
size_gib = [math]::Round($loaded.size / 1GB, 1)
gpu_percent = if ($loaded.size) { [math]::Round(100 * $loaded.size_vram / $loaded.size) } else { $null }
}

# Code benchmark
$t0 = Get-Date
$code = foreach ($cp in $codePrompts) {
$r, $w = Invoke-Timed (New-Body $cp 512)
$m = Measure-Reply $r $w; $m.prompt = $cp
Write-Host ("  code: {0} tok/s decode, {1} tokens" -f $m.decode_tok_s, $m.output_tokens)
[pscustomobject]$m
}
$phases += [pscustomobject]@{ model = $model; phase = 'code'; start = $t0; end = Get-Date }

# Long-prompt benchmark: calibrate characters per token, then build the prompt just under the target
# A large slice: token density varies a lot between files, and Ollama silently cuts prompts longer than num_ctx
$sample = $corpus.Substring(0, [math]::Min($p.num_ctx * 3, $corpus.Length))
$r, $w = Invoke-Timed (New-Body ("Calibration $(Get-Random)`n" + $sample) 1)
$charsPerToken = $sample.Length / $r.prompt_eval_count
$chars = [int]($p.long_tokens * $charsPerToken * 0.97)
$text = $corpus
while ($text.Length -lt $chars) { $text += $corpus }
$question = "`n`nQuestion: Describe what the files above define, as a numbered list covering every file."
$longPrompt = "Session $([guid]::NewGuid())`n" + $text.Substring(0, $chars) + $question
Start-Sleep 5
$t0 = Get-Date
$r, $w = Invoke-Timed (New-Body $longPrompt 384)
$long = Measure-Reply $r $w
$long.chars_per_token = [math]::Round($charsPerToken, 2)
# Ollama keeps only the end of an over-long prompt (about half the context); a short count means that happened
$long.truncated = $r.prompt_eval_count -lt 0.8 * $p.long_tokens
if ($long.truncated) { Write-Warning "$model long prompt was truncated to $($r.prompt_eval_count) tokens" }
$phases += [pscustomobject]@{ model = $model; phase = 'long'; start = $t0; end = Get-Date }
Write-Host ("  long: {0} prompt tokens, {1} tok/s prefill, {2} tok/s decode, {3} s to first token" -f $long.prompt_tokens, $long.prefill_tok_s, $long.decode_tok_s, $long.first_token_s)

$modelInfo.code = $code
$modelInfo.code_mean_decode_tok_s = [math]::Round(($code | Measure-Object decode_tok_s -Average).Average, 2)
$modelInfo.long = [pscustomobject]$long
$results += [pscustomobject]$modelInfo
Start-Sleep 5
}
}
finally {
Stop-Process -Id $sampler.Id -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
Unload-All
}

# GPU summary per phase from the nvidia-smi samples
$samples = Get-Content $gpuCsv | Where-Object { $_ -match '^\d{4}/' } | ForEach-Object {
$f = $_.Split(',') | ForEach-Object { $_.Trim() }
[pscustomobject]@{
time = [datetime]::ParseExact($f[0], 'yyyy/MM/dd HH:mm:ss.fff', $null)
power_w = [double]$f[1]; temp_c = [int]$f[2]; clock_mhz = [int]$f[3]; mem_mib = [int]$f[4]
}
}
$gpuPhases = foreach ($ph in $phases) {
$s = @($samples | Where-Object { $_.time -ge $ph.start -and $_.time -le $ph.end })
if (-not $s.Count) { continue }
[pscustomobject][ordered]@{
model = $ph.model; phase = $ph.phase; seconds = [math]::Round(($ph.end - $ph.start).TotalSeconds, 1)
avg_power_w = [math]::Round(($s | Measure-Object power_w -Average).Average, 1)
max_power_w = ($s | Measure-Object power_w -Maximum).Maximum
max_temp_c = ($s | Measure-Object temp_c -Maximum).Maximum
avg_clock_mhz = [math]::Round(($s | Measure-Object clock_mhz -Average).Average)
max_mem_mib = ($s | Measure-Object mem_mib -Maximum).Maximum
}
}

$battery = Get-CimInstance Win32_Battery -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Select-Object -First 1
$out = [ordered]@{
computer = $env:COMPUTERNAME; date = (Get-Date).ToString('s')
ollama_version = (Invoke-RestMethod ($Api + '/api/version')).version
gpu = $gpuInfo
power_scheme = (powercfg /getactivescheme) -replace '^.*\((.*)\)\s*$', '$1'
on_ac_power = if ($battery) { $battery.BatteryStatus -eq 2 } else { $null }
models = $results
gpu_phases = $gpuPhases
}
$json = $out | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 8
[IO.File]::WriteAllText("$base.json", $json, (New-Object Text.UTF8Encoding $false))
$gpuPhases | Format-Table -AutoSize | Out-String | Write-Host
Write-Host "Results: $base.json"
Write-Host "GPU samples: $gpuCsv"
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building financial software is not just about shipping features, integrating APIs, or getting an application approved by an app store. It also requires clear agreements about ownership, compensation, accountability, and what happens when the people building the system no longer agree.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I learned this through my involvement in Amalfi, a blockchain-based financial platform now publicly available as Amalfi Global Bank.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google Play: &lt;a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.amalfi.banking" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.amalfi.banking&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Apple App Store: &lt;a href="https://apps.apple.com/br/app/amalfi-global-bank/id6755686818" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://apps.apple.com/br/app/amalfi-global-bank/id6755686818&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My work involved significant technical components of the platform. Over time, however, the professional relationship behind that work became the subject of an unresolved dispute involving outstanding financial matters, intellectual property rights, and the terms under which I could formally disengage from the project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where things became difficult.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In our communications, I was repeatedly described not merely as a developer, but as a partner who would help lead technology and future development. There are written messages documenting those conversations, as well as discussions acknowledging outstanding matters that still needed to be resolved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yet the relationship never reached a definitive written settlement addressing all the issues now in dispute.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My position is that the technical work, the financial obligations, and the rights associated with the software need to be addressed formally. The other party disputes aspects of my account. No final agreement has resolved the disagreement, and I am pursuing the appropriate formal channels rather than presenting contested claims as established legal findings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I am not publishing this to encourage harassment, interfere with customers, or ask anyone to take sides without examining the facts. I am publishing it because there is a broader engineering lesson here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;If a company depends on critical software, the relationship with the people who build it cannot be managed through informal promises indefinitely.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A project can have a public application, a product roadmap, external integrations, and a compelling financial narrative while the agreements governing its underlying engineering work remain incomplete.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is a governance problem, not merely a personal disagreement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For engineers working on fintech, blockchain infrastructure, or any security-sensitive system, I would take several lessons from this experience:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Define compensation, equity, and partnership status in writing. They are different arrangements and should never be left ambiguous.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Establish software ownership, licensing, and assignment terms before a dispute arises.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Record deliverables, acceptance criteria, dependencies, and external blockers in a way that both parties can audit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Define what happens when a contributor leaves, including access revocation, handover, ongoing support, and the rights to existing work.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Treat intellectual property and engineering continuity as first-class governance concerns, not paperwork to postpone until the product succeeds.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I still believe the engineering work should be evaluated on its actual merits. I also believe that financial and intellectual property disputes should be resolved through evidence, proper agreements, and the appropriate legal processes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But technical professionals should not have to rely solely on trust and informal assurances when building infrastructure that a business intends to operate commercially.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ship the software. Document the work. Define the rights. Put the agreement in writing before success makes the ambiguity expensive.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That lesson cost me more than I expected to learn, and I hope other engineers can learn it without having to go through the same experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Short answer: use a unified gateway for supplier-invoice extraction when the requirement is one credential, one chat contract, and controlled fallback across OpenAI, Claude, and Gemini. Keep the recommendation conditional: quality comes before latency, and fallback is useful only when both models return the same structured fields. Check EU and US processing requirements separately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The incident pattern is familiar even without a dramatic outage. An invoice worker gets a rate-limit response, retries another model, and hands the support system valid-looking prose instead of seven usable fields. The dashboard is green because requests completed. The page should have asked a narrower question: which model answered, what schema did it satisfy, and did the retry preserve the invoice contract?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What should the gateway solve before it routes anything?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The bounded job is extracting &lt;code&gt;supplier_name&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;invoice_number&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;invoice_date&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;currency&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;subtotal&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;tax&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;total&lt;/code&gt; from supplier invoices for a customer-support workflow. That is a text workload. The application should treat the output as data, not as a helpful paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I start with a fixed application schema, then choose a primary model and a fallback from the current catalog. I initially thought the fallback label was the important part; I was wrong. A single chat endpoint reduces adapter code, while a model catalog and metadata endpoint make the decision inspectable: the worker can discover available model IDs before sending a request, and an operator can compare the selected model with the one that actually returned the response.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the invariant: fallback must preserve the output contract. A second answer is not a successful fallback if it changes &lt;code&gt;invoice_date&lt;/code&gt; into free text, drops &lt;code&gt;tax&lt;/code&gt;, or returns a refusal that the caller mistakes for JSON.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction matters at 3am.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Should one gateway API serve OpenAI Claude and Gemini?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are four reasonable shapes for this job. None is universally best.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Strength&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Boundary&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Suitable case&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;OpenAI direct API&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Direct access to one model family&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The application owns other credentials, adapters, and fallback logic&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;OpenAI is the deliberate boundary&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Anthropic Claude direct API&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Direct Claude request semantics&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cross-vendor routing remains application code&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Claude is the deliberate boundary&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Google Gemini direct API&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Direct Gemini request semantics&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One-key fallback is not supplied by the direct integration&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Google is already the platform boundary&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Unified gateway&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One key and one chat-shaped contract across vendors&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Catalog, regions, limits, and failure behavior need review&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Standard text extraction needs controlled choice&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The difference is operational ownership, not a beauty contest. Direct APIs make sense when one vendor's native controls are non-negotiable. A gateway makes more sense when the actual requirement is one key across three vendors and the team does not want three credential lifecycles embedded in every worker.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrai's verified advantage is one key and one bill: one credential removes per-vendor credential and reconciliation work from this workflow. Infrai also exposes one REST API, so the worker needs no SDK or client-library version to babysit. Its public, self-describing discovery surface is another useful check: the worker can inspect the catalog and schemas before it treats a model as a valid fallback. The broader platform reports 295 routes across 20 modules, but this worker should depend only on the small chat and model-catalog contract it actually tests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fit has limits. Audio transcription is unavailable, realtime voice sessions are pending and limited to the western region, and there is no dedicated moderation endpoint. Infrai is not a fit when those capabilities are the product requirement; choose a direct specialist integration instead. That is a real trade-off, not a footnote. For moderation, use a chat model with schema-based JSON output as the policy path. Do not add an unavailable capability to an invoice fallback chain just because its route shape appears in documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How should the fallback protect quality and rate limits?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Classify the failure before changing models. A &lt;code&gt;429&lt;/code&gt; means back off and honor &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt;; a schema failure means validate the response and decide whether another model is allowed; an ordinary client error should surface the response body rather than disappear into a generic retry. A timeout may justify a fallback, but only while the request is still safe to repeat.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The following Go program discovers the model list, retries rate limits with exponential backoff, checks non-success responses, and sends the identical extraction prompt to a primary and then a fallback. Model IDs come from the environment because catalog availability is live state. Set &lt;code&gt;INFRAI_BASE_URL&lt;/code&gt; to the service's documented &lt;code&gt;/v1&lt;/code&gt; base; keeping it configurable also makes a staging endpoint possible.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"bytes"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"context"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"encoding/json"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"fmt"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"io"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"net/http"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"os"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"strconv"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"strings"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"time"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

